      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, im putting together a syllabus for a grad seminar on colonial literature and i wanted to include joseph conrad's heart of darkness. can you give me a quick summary of the plot and maybe some of the main themes? specifically looking for how the book handles the depictions of the people in the congo.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "\"Heart of Darkness\" follows Marlow, a representative of European imperialism, who embarks on a journey up the Congo River to meet Kurtz, a man believed to have achieved great success in Africa. Marlow returns, disillusioned, and experiences the brutal reality of colonial exploitation, revealing Kurtz's descent into madness and madness of colonial enterprise in Europe. Key themes include the darkness within humanity, colonial exploitation, civilization versus savagery, and isolation. The African people are depicted as exotic, primitive, and often viewed as background to European narrative, which reflect contemporary European attitudes and reinforce notions of racial superiority and imperialism. Colonial depictions are complex, reflective of both the darkness marred by the conquests and Marlow's complicity, as thirty-four-year-old man struggling to comprehend the atrocities wreaked upon the natives by the system he represents.",
